</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/974e88c4-89be-11f1-aa4d-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C39Q92L.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/974e88c4-89be-11f1-aa4d-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C39Q92L.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">70d66b12-899b-11f1-b6e6-9393e3857403</guid><pubDate>Mon, 27 Jul 2026 15:12:48 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/974e88c4-89be-11f1-aa4d-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C39Q92L.jpg">AFP - SARAH MEYSSONNIER</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>Threatened by fire, Bordeaux is 'ready for anything': mayor</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/france/20260727-threatened-by-fire-bordeaux-is-ready-for-anything-mayor</link><description>
The mayor of Bordeaux in southwest France said on Sunday authorities had no current plans to evacuate the UNESCO-listed city due to raging forest fires but were "ready for anything." 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5643fa6c-8838-11f1-887c-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-25T141114Z-776257927-RC2XKMARM7UI-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-EVACUATION.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5643fa6c-8838-11f1-887c-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-25T141114Z-776257927-RC2XKMARM7UI-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-EVACUATION.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">82fc7b50-88fc-11f1-8151-73a8bbe5631a</guid><pubDate>Mon, 27 Jul 2026 06:37:12 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5643fa6c-8838-11f1-887c-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-25T141114Z-776257927-RC2XKMARM7UI-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-EVACUATION.jpg">REUTERS - Manon Cruz</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>How slavery money built the Élysée, France’s presidential residence</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/france/20260726-how-slavery-money-built-the-%C3%A9lys%C3%A9e-france-s-presidential-residence</link><description>
What would become the Élysée Palace, the official residence of the French president, was built three centuries ago with a fortune amassed in the slave trade. As France grapples with its legacy of slavery, calls are mounting for the country to acknowledge this and other traces of the past.
